| A value's truth, as `if`, `assert`, `not`, `and` and `or` read it: `false`,
| `null`, `0` and any `Exception` are false, and everything else is true. An
| empty string, array or object is not a failure, so all three are true.
assert true
assert not false
assert not null
assert not 0
assert 1
assert -1
assert 0.5
assert "false"
assert ""
assert []
assert {}
assert [0]
assert { a = 1 }

| The reason the rule exists: the answer to an emit that could not do the
| work. `not n` is the whole of "it did not work", where the check written
| out is `n ∈ Exception`.
emit Length { value = 5 } to core get n
assert n ∈ Exception
assert not n

let taken = 0
if n {
    taken = 1
}
assert taken = 0

if not n {
    taken = 2
}
assert taken = 2

| A particle that did work is an ordinary object, so it is simply true.
emit Length { value = [1, 2] } to core get ok
assert ok
assert ok.value = 2

| `and`/`or` read both sides the same way, and always answer a Bool — neither
| ever hands an operand back.
assert (0 or "x") = true
assert (0 and "x") = false
assert (n or true) = true
assert (n and true) = false
assert ("a" and [1]) = true
assert (null or 0) = false
